<p>The White House said China agreed to buy at least $17 billion in U.S. agricultural products annually in 2026, 2027 and 2028 after President Trump met Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing last week. The White House said the commitment is separate from an October 2025 soybean purchase agreement, under which China committed to buy 25 million metric tons of U.S. soybeans annually through 2028 as part of a broader trade truce. The White House also said the governments agreed to establish U.S.-China boards of trade and investment. It said China agreed to an initial purchase of 200 Boeing aircraft, and that both leaders agreed Iran should not obtain a nuclear weapon and that the Strait of Hormuz should reopen without tolls. China’s Commerce Ministry said the sides reached preliminary results on lowering some tariffs and expanding farm trade, but said negotiating teams were still working through details; the White House fact sheet did not specify tariff cuts.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/-lTRliEAu6EOU-BCY8HUPzckTW5xZ8yH2PAxatUjjN6H4wCHLMHvyJwcHVB9mB5YC-aMJDKBOmnlhAJnFZJKkeFmK3Lut9OhzsemRG0psikuSkUrafQFuxZ3XNHK8mmriRjG-xZDXW1dvo7X-PrVguJspLF6UMVWa68qdv3k-BdPRWYi7ynQS5XxzudId3YpYKQCAn_meLNlDqwJCHc4Ni67pj0JvtTm0X1Wnn05_zKjQAk4uQ7PE6_agIrEvfhW3KEiP50i4OxFuoLDCm4IDAEbzFRUtPAu2kZbfA9-5vrkoKMB8A' alt='President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping during U.S.-China meetings in Beijing.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Trade decline: U.S. agricultural exports to China fell 65.7% year on year to $8.4 billion in 2025 after rounds of tit-for-tat tariffs.</li><li>Meat access: China renewed expired registrations for more than 400 U.S. beef facilities, added new listings and said it would resume poultry imports from U.S. states it considers free of avian influenza.</li><li>Non-tariff barriers: China’s readout said the sides would resolve or substantially advance some agricultural non-tariff barriers and market-access issues.</li><li>Summit expectations: Politico said Trump had pitched the Beijing summit in March as a “monumental event”.</li><li>Diplomatic follow-up: The White House said both countries would support each other as hosts of the G20 and APEC summits.</li></ul><blockquote>We expect to also see an agreement for double-digit billion purchases of ags over the next three years, per year, coming out of this visit - Jamieson Greer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: The administration said the package would open markets for U.S. goods and support high-paying American jobs. (<a href='https://www.livemint.com/news/world/200-boeing-aircraft-17-billion-in-farm-imports-what-donald-trump-negotiated-for-us-with-china-11779071954261.html'>Livemint</a>)</li><li>China Commerce Ministry: Beijing’s readout emphasized that the understandings were preliminary and that negotiating teams were still working through details, especially on tariff cuts. (<a href='https://fortune.com/2026/05/17/us-china-purchase-17-billion-agricultural-goods-trump-xi-jinping-summit/'>Fortune</a>)</li><li>U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer: Greer said renewed Chinese demand was important for farmers facing a year of collapsing soybean exports, lower crop prices and higher production costs. (<a href='https://www.michiganagtoday.com/2026/05/17/trump-administration-china-set-to-buy-double-digit-billions-in-us-ag-products/'>Michigan Ag Today</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/5/18/us-says-china-to-buy-billions-in-agricultural-goods-after-trump-xi-talks'>US says China to buy billions in agricultural goods after Trump-Xi talks</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/aed42b42-0ca2-4104-848a-c8bca33fd837'>White House says Trump and Xi agreed on ‘board of trade’ at summit</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1tfz5hm/china_to_buy_at_least_17_billion_in_us/'>China to buy at least $17 billion in US agricultural products annually, White